  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 335 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nNortheasterly surface flow will gradually veer to an easterly \ndirection by Thursday as the axis of surface ridging across the \nsoutheastern United States slides eastward into the western Atlantic \nwaters. This will focus the best diurnal rain chances across the \nwestern half of the peninsula during the second half of the work \nweek, although overnight and morning activity will still be a \npossibility along the east coast of South Florida given the breezy \neasterly flow and continued moisture advection. Mid-level ridging \nbriefly established across the southeastern United States during the \nearly to mid portion of the work week will quickly flatten by \nThursday as the next lobe of troughing slides eastward across the \nnorthern United States. As the positively tilted trough amplifies \nand digs across the eastern United States, a strengthening surface \nlow will strengthen and push east-northeastward in tandem. Model \nguidance remains in agreement that a surface frontal boundary \nattached to the aforementioned surface low will push southwards \nacross the eastern United States in response and arrive in the \nvicinity of the Florida Peninsula during the upcoming weekend. There \nremains a great amount of variability/ uncertainty in model \nsolutions of exact frontal boundary placement) from run to run and \nmodel member to model member (ensemble vs deterministic). Model \nguidance has trended away from a full passage of the boundary across \nthe region and now depicts an initial stall of the boundary somewhere \nacross the Florida Peninsula. \n\nThe placement of the aforementioned frontal boundary in relation to \nSouth Florida, surface high pressure axis to the north, and any \nmesoscale coastal convergence will have a direct influence on \npotential rainfall totals, wind gusts, and the severity of any \nmarine/coastal impacts across the region during the upcoming weekend \ninto next week. Forecasted precipitable water and low level RH \nvalues (via forecast soundings) during this time period appear to \napproach the daily max for this time of year, indicative of an \nairmass that is saturated in the low level boundary layer. 500mb \ntemperatures are also forecast to be on the warmer side which may \nsupport an expansive warm cloud layer. Outside of the heavy rain, \nguidance has also been consistent in showing the enhancement of a \npressure gradient across the western Atlantic and South Florida. \nThis would act to increase wind speeds across the area, resulting in \ngusty conditions and potential marine and coastal impacts. The \nforecast remains highly uncertain and fluid, stay tuned for a \nclearer idea of what may happen as we progress through the \nremainder of the work week. Forecast high temperatures will remain \nstatus quo through the end of the work week with values in the upper \n80s to low 90s possible each afternoon. Apparent temperatures will \nalso rebound with increased moisture, reaching the upper 90s to low \n100s on Thursday and Friday. An increase in wind and rain chances \nmay keep ambient and apparent temperatures lower beginning during \nthe upcoming weekend.",
